{
  "sim_type": "excel",
  "title": "Normalise TCS Earnings: Strip One-Offs to Find Real Profit",
  "scenario": "TCS reported FY2024 PAT of ₹42,150 crore, but the finance team notes exceptional items: a ₹8,500 crore one-time gain on the sale of a subsidiary stake, a ₹2,100 crore impairment charge on legacy infrastructure, and a ₹1,200 crore one-time tax settlement (gain). Your Mumbai desk must strip these to find the true recurring earnings — the number to value the company on.",
  "instructions": "Identify each non-recurring item, compute its after-tax impact at 23% tax rate, and build normalised PAT by removing gains and adding back losses, applying the frequency test to flag recurring 'exceptionals' that should not be adjusted.",
  "learning_objective": "Compute normalised earnings by stripping after-tax non-recurring gains and losses in both directions, apply the frequency test to reclassify mislabelled exceptionals, and compute the honest normalised P/E that reveals true valuation.",
  "hints": [
    "Start with reported PAT; identify which items are truly one-time (appear once) versus recurring (appear yearly). After-tax means multiply the pre-tax amount by (1 − tax rate).",
    "Non-recurring gains reduce normalised earnings (subtract from reported), non-recurring losses increase it (add back). A ₹1,200 crore pre-tax tax benefit at 23% tax is roughly ₹924 crore after-tax; subtract it from reported PAT.",
    "The impairment charge: if it's genuinely one-time, add back its after-tax amount. The subsidiary stake sale: if it's a genuine disposal (not part of normal operations), strip the after-tax gain. Then compute normalised P/E using normalised earnings as the denominator."
  ],
